{
  "reviewer": "abstract_conclusion_consistency_auditor",
  "paper_id": "bicycle-infrastructure-paper",
  "run_status": "ok",
  "summary": "The abstract, introduction and conclusion were checked against the body, using page text and the parsed full text. The headline counts reconcile with the body: 3,187 articles, 92 openings, 43 projects, 14 of 15 counties, and 29 projects dated to the day or month (19 + 10). Twelve problems were found. Three of the twelve concern the pilot validation and the full run. The abstract and conclusion report the 97 percent human–LLM agreement without the body's small-sample caveat (3 relevant articles out of 34). They also report the 92-article and 43-project results without saying that the full run was not checked against human coding. One claim is stale: the abstract's 'only two actual openings' conflicts with Table 2 footnote c, which says the Grettefoss Bridge opened on 3 November. The abstract also says the data was shown to be linkable to housing listings, although the body says no linkage has been done. The conclusion and introduction describe research uses (cycling behaviour, house prices, infrastructure types) that the body says still need further work. The remaining findings concern the NVDB generalisation, the keyword-search claim, coverage and selection caveats, and the selective date precision that Section 6.3 reports. One body-to-body inconsistency concerns the Stord project (footnote 3 vs Section 4.1). The parser-quality guidance was followed: page text was used throughout, and the structured table files were not used as evidence.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "CONSIST-001",
      "category": "abstract_overstatement",
      "finding_summary": "The abstract reports the 97 percent human–LLM agreement as equal to human–human agreement without the body's caveat that the pilot has only 34 articles, 3 of them relevant, and is too small for any comparison of the two rates.",
